-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/dfc1386f-e2f7-4d4a-8a1c-8c81c31ee674
-- title:
--   Theorem 5.4 — identification of the limiting dynamics and the limiting value (5.8)
-- statement:
--   Assume Assumption A, and let $(\alpha^n)$ be an arbitrary sequence of profiles of admissible closed-loop controls with state processes (5.1) and empirical measure flows $\mu^n$. Suppose a subsequence $(\mu^{n_k})$ converges in law in $C([0,T];\mathcal P(\mathbb R^d))$ to $(\mu_t)_{t\in[0,T]}$. Then there is a semi-Markov $\Lambda^*:[0,T]\times\mathbb R^d\times C([0,T];\mathcal P(\mathbb R^d))\to\mathcal P(A)$ such that, on a probability space carrying $\mu$, there are continuous processes $X$ and $W$ with
--
--   1. (i) $X_0$, $W$ and $\mu$ independent;
--   2. (ii) $W$ a Brownian motion with respect to the completed filtration $\mathbb F$ generated by $(X_0,\mu_t,W_t)_{t\in[0,T]}$;
--   3. (iii) $X$ adapted to the completion of $\mathbb F$, with $X_0\sim\lambda$;
--   4. (iv) $dX_t=\int_A b(t,X_t,\mu_t,a)\Lambda^*(t,X_t,\mu)(da)\,dt+dW_t$;
--   5. (v) $\mu_t=\mathcal L(X_t\mid\mathcal F^\mu_t)$ a.s. for each $t$, $\mathcal F^\mu_t=\sigma(\mu_s:s\le t)$;
--
--   and moreover, along a further subsequence $(n_{k_j})_j$,
--   $$\lim_{j\to\infty}\frac1{n_{k_j}}\sum_{i=1}^{n_{k_j}}J^{n_{k_j}}_i(\alpha^{n_{k_j},1},\dots,\alpha^{n_{k_j},n_{k_j}})=\mathbb E\Big[\int_0^T\int_A f(t,X_t,\mu_t,a)\Lambda^*(t,X_t,\mu)(da)\,dt+g(X_T,\mu_T)\Big]. \tag{5.8}$$
--
--   The paper prints the limit (5.8) along the whole subsequence $(n_k)$; we state it along a further subsequence, because the printed claim is false for an arbitrary sequence of controls and the proof (p. 28, "We may then pass to a further subsequence") gives exactly the corrected form. Counterexample to the printed form: $A=[0,1]$, $b=0$, $f(t,x,m,a)=a$, $g=0$ and $\alpha^{n,i}\equiv n \bmod 2$; the flows $\mu^n$ do not depend on the controls and converge in law, while the average payoff equals $T\,(n \bmod 2)$ and has no limit. The use of (5.8) in Section 5.5 and in the proof of Theorem 3.9 only needs the corrected form, since Section 5.5 relabels the subsequence.
--
--   This proves properties (1)–(4) and (6) of a weak RMFE at every limit point, without using the Nash property.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Term $n$ of the sequence is the game with $n+1$ players, so $n_k$ players becomes $\varphi(k)+1$ players. "Converges in law to $\mu$" is convergence of the laws to a probability measure $\nu$; "by extending the probability space if needed" is the existence of a probability space carrying a flow $\mu$ with law $\nu$ and the processes $X$, $W$. The payoffs $J^{n}_i$ are computed on the given weak solutions of the $n$-player systems (any solution gives the same value). The SDE (iv) is pathwise.
-- source:
--   Lacker, On the convergence of closed-loop Nash equilibria to the mean field game limit, arXiv:1808.02745v1, p. 28, Theorem 5.4

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ClosedLoopMFG_Limit_Model
import Definitions.Def_ClosedLoopMFG_Limit_Game
import Definitions.Def_ClosedLoopMFG_Limit_Equilibrium
import Definitions.Def_ClosedLoopMFG_Limit_Relaxed

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory Filter Topology
open scoped ENNReal NNReal

namespace ClosedLoopMFG.Limit

/-- **Theorem 5.4** (p. 28). For an arbitrary sequence of admissible profiles `αⁿ` (term `n` is
the game with `n + 1` players), if the flows `μ^{n_k}` converge in law along a subsequence to a
law `ν`, then there are a `P(A)`-valued semi-Markov `Λ*` and a probability space carrying a flow
`μ` with law `ν` and processes `X`, `W` satisfying (i)–(v), and the average payoffs converge to
the limit value (5.8) along a further subsequence `n_{k_j}` (the paper prints the limit (5.8) along
the whole subsequence `n_k`; its proof passes to a further subsequence, and without it the claim is
false: `b = 0`, `f(t, x, m, a) = a`, `A = [0, 1]`, `α^{n,i} ≡ n mod 2`). -/
theorem theorem_5_4 {d : ℕ} {T : ℝ≥0} (hT : 0 < T) {EA : Type} [NormedAddCommGroup EA]
    [NormedSpace ℝ EA] [MeasurableSpace EA] [BorelSpace EA] (A : Set EA) (lam : PR d)
    (b : ℝ → E d → PR d → EA → E d) (f : ℝ → E d → PR d → EA → ℝ) (g : E d → PR d → ℝ)
    (hA : AssumptionA T A b f g)
    (α : (n : ℕ) → Fin (n + 1) → ℝ → (Fin (n + 1) → Path d T) → EA)
    (hα : ∀ n i, IsAdmissible A (α n i))
    (S : (n : ℕ) → NSol (n + 1) d T lam (drift b (α n)))
    (φ : ℕ → ℕ) (hφ : StrictMono φ) (ν : ProbabilityMeasure (Flow d T))
    (hν : Tendsto (fun k => (S (φ k)).law) atTop (𝓝 ν)) :
    ∃ Λ : ℝ → E d → Flow d T → PA A, IsSemiMarkov Λ ∧
      ∃ (Ω : Type) (_ : MeasurableSpace Ω) (P : Measure Ω) (W : ℝ≥0 → Ω → E d)
        (X : Ω → Path d T) (μ : Ω → Flow d T),
        P.map μ = (ν : Measure (Flow d T)) ∧ IsRelaxedRealization lam b Λ P W X μ ∧
        ∃ ψ : ℕ → ℕ, StrictMono ψ ∧
          Tendsto (fun j => avgPayoff (S (φ (ψ j))) f g (α (φ (ψ j)))) atTop
            (𝓝 (reward P X μ (relaxRun f Λ) g)) := by sorry

end ClosedLoopMFG.Limit
